sections.text:
- Primary client users: the DS maintainers (design + engineering).
- Primary buyer: Head of Design / Front-end.
- Primary internal users: sellers and delivery teams.
- Intended outcomes (all hypotheses, to measure in the pilot):
  - fewer design↔code mismatches;
  - AI output on-system;
  - changes reviewed in both tools;
  - the client runs the system without us.

- MVP:
  - the system contract (v0);
  - the React adapter (one profile);
  - the panel with Overview, Components, Tokens, Changes, Share;
  - suggestions from existing audit rules;
  - edits to tokens and component guidance/metadata as PRs;
  - Figma change set via the existing plugin;
  - Path A wired through;
  - Path B scan (read-only) + service adaptation for one pilot.
- Later:
  - component code edits;
  - adapters for Tailwind/shadcn, MUI/Ant, Tokens Studio, zeroheight;
  - the Govern dashboard (297:285 screen 8);
  - hosted multi-user;
  - non-React stacks (tokens + rules only);
  - public showcase (D8).
- Excluded:
  - merging libraries or grammars;
  - auto-merge or auto-resolution of drift;
  - "any design system" claims;
  - client brands in shared Nagarro files.

- Onboarding:
  - A: template + wizard;
  - B: read-only access to repo + Figma, then the scan.
- Adaptation: contract + adapters + metadata, by us.
- Handover: checklist of what they own; a named governance owner (the decision map says "strongest predictor"); a training session on the panel.
- Maintenance:
  - they run it;
  - we ship panel/adapter versions;
  - Support covers upgrades and reviews.
- Panel delivery model: template per client now, package before the third client (already decided).

- Dependencies:
  - GitHub access for PRs (DSN uses the gh CLI, O);
  - Figma plan: the plugin works on any paid plan, Code Connect publishing needs Organization/Enterprise (O);
  - for agent writes to Figma, a Full seat (Figma docs).
- Open decisions: see the brief.
- Risks:
  1. brownfield adaptation is too bespoke;
  2. capacity (~1 day/week each);
  3. agent quality untested end to end;
  4. React-only limits the market;
  5. client IP leaking into shared assets (has happened once: assa_abloy_test);
  6. demoing a proposed step as working.

- Storybook runs inside the repo and reads real stories. Its 10.x component manifest + MCP give agents structured component data, but only for React, in preview. The "editable Storybook" idea is credible precedent, and we should *consume* Storybook where it exists, not compete with it.
- Primer (GitHub) handles change control through PRs and changesets. We reuse PR review instead of inventing approval.
- Carbon combines token tiers with themes. Our modes-vs-tiers split (Starter Pack vs DSN) is expressible in one contract without picking a winner.
- DTCG / Style Dictionary is the interchange format. DSN and ASSA already export DTCG-style JSON; the Starter Pack does not (O). The ASSA DTCG puts modes inside $value, which is not standard (O).
- Polaris moving from React to web components is a warning that a React-only adapter is a market limit, not a permanent truth.
- Market:
  - zeroheight's MCP gives read access (paid plans);
  - Knapsack's MCP links tokens, components and docs to AI tools;
  - Supernova's Editor MCP adds writes (beta);
  - Figma opened the canvas to agents with use_figma and skills (24 Mar 2026);
  - theme tools: shadcn/studio, tweakcn.

  Conclusion: reading a system with AI and theming it are commodity. Reviewed, two-sided change with gates, inside the client's repo, plus a method for messy systems is where we can stand out. *Inferred from the scan; no competitor was tested.*
- NN/g: only secondhand sources were found (Kate Moran on the *Design Systems* podcast: humans stay essential for brand consistency in generative UI). They support keeping human review in every write path. Verify on nngroup.com before quoting.

Assumption / question · How to check · Signal

Clients value a *retained* panel over a one-off clean-up · Demo test with 1–2 friendly clients · They ask who maintains it, or ask to keep it
Our three systems fit one contract · Stage 0 · % of tokens/components mapped without renaming
A public React DS can be adapted mostly by config · Stage 1 · Adapter needs config only vs new code
Drift findings are trustworthy · Manual check of a sample · Precision on the sample
Sellers can run the demo without the practice · Run with 3–5 colleagues · They retell the offer in their words
Repo-local editing is acceptable to client security · Ask the pilot client's engineering · Allowed in dev / CI only
One owner can carry the panel at ~1 day/week · Phase 1 burn · Missed gates

Success measures to track (no baselines exist; record the first value as the baseline):
- time from first call to a booked audit;
- demo-to-audit conversion;
- drift items open over time per client;
- percentage of components with complete metadata;
- percentage of PRs that touch both Figma and code;
- agent output passing the checks.
